response.answer:
Here is the comparison at a high level, based on the AI Act provisions on **providers of high-risk AI systems** and **deployers of high-risk AI systems**.

| Aspect | **Provider** | **Deployer** |
|---|---|---|
| Core role | Must ensure the system is compliant before it is placed on the market or put into service. | Must use the system according to the instructions for use and manage its operation in deployment. |
| Compliance focus | Full lifecycle compliance with the high-risk requirements in Section 2. | Correct use, oversight, monitoring, and certain operational duties during use. |
| Documentation / records | Keeps the required documentation and, when under control, the system logs. | Keeps logs under its control for at least six months, unless law provides otherwise. |
| Conformity assessment / CE / registration | Carries out the conformity assessment, draws up the EU declaration of conformity, affixes the CE marking, and complies with registration obligations. | Public authorities and Union bodies must comply with registration obligations and must not use an unregistered system in the EU database. |
| Human oversight | Must build the system so that it supports compliance with the requirements, including human oversight requirements. | Must assign human oversight to competent, trained and supported natural persons. |
| Incident / risk handling | Must take necessary corrective actions and provide information as required. | Must monitor operation, inform provider/distributor and authorities if the system may present a risk, suspend use, and report serious incidents. |
| Workplace duty | Not stated in the deployer article. | Employers must inform workers’ representatives and affected workers before putting the system into service or using it at the workplace. |
| Additional special duties | Must ensure accessibility requirements are met. | Additional special rules apply for data protection impact assessments and, for post-remote biometric identification, authorisation, documentation, annual reports, and limits on use. |

## Provider obligations
Providers of high-risk AI systems must:
- ensure the system complies with the requirements in Section 2;
- indicate their name and contact address on the system, packaging, or documentation;
- have a **quality management system** in place;
- keep the required documentation;
- keep automatically generated logs when under their control;
- ensure the relevant conformity assessment is carried out before placing the system on the market or putting it into service;
- draw up the EU declaration of conformity;
- affix the CE marking;
- comply with registration obligations;
- take corrective actions and provide information as required;
- demonstrate conformity to a national competent authority on reasoned request;
- ensure compliance with accessibility requirements. 

## Deployer obligations
Deployers of high-risk AI systems must:
- take appropriate technical and organisational measures to use the system in accordance with the instructions for use;
- assign human oversight to competent, trained and supported natural persons;
- ensure relevant and sufficiently representative input data, to the extent they control input data;
- monitor operation and, where relevant, inform providers;
- if they have reason to consider the system may present a risk, inform the provider or distributor and the relevant market surveillance authority without undue delay, and suspend use;
- if they identify a serious incident, immediately inform the provider, then the importer or distributor and the relevant authorities;
- keep logs under their control for at least six months;
- if they are employers, inform workers’ representatives and affected workers before workplace use;
- if they are public authorities or Union institutions, bodies, offices or agencies, comply with registration obligations and do not use an unregistered system;
- use the Article 13 information for any required data protection impact assessment;
- for post-remote biometric identification, obtain ex ante authorisation (or within 48 hours in the specified alternative), comply with use limits, document each use, and submit annual reports. 

In short: the **provider** is primarily responsible for designing, validating, documenting, registering and placing a compliant high-risk AI system on the market; the **deployer** is primarily responsible for safe, instructed, monitored and properly documented use of that system in operation.

